#endif :::Technical Documentation:The source code for the project can be found in the associated Github repository.Schematic{#fig-schematic}?@fig-schematic shows the electical layout of the design. Since our output can’t necessarily drive a speaker, an audio amplifier is needed for the speaker to properly read the GPIO output. Additionally, a potentiometer serves as volume control.Results and DiscussionThe overall design and construction was successful as confirmed via physical circuitry and the successful upload of the solution to the MCU. I was able to accomplish all tasks outlined in the beginning of this lab report and utilize some interesting logic that I didn’t see other peers using.ConclusionThe design successfully programmed the speaker to play two tunes. I learned a lot about communicating with the MCU using a reference manual and datasheet and manipulating the values of registers to essentially get a MAX COUNT.AI Prototype Summary{#fig-aisynthesis}::: {.callout-note collapse=“true” title=“Initial Prompt”}Timers to Use (220 Hz to 1 kHz)The STM32L432KC includes several general-purpose and advanced timers suitable for signal and PWM generation:TIM2 (32-bit): High-precision general-purpose timer. Its 32-bit width provides a massive counting range, making low frequencies easily reachable without risk of counter overflow.TIM1 (16-bit): Advanced-control timer. Fully capable, but includes extra complex features (break inputs, dead-time) that are unnecessary for basic audio/PWM signals.TIM15 & TIM16 (16-bit): General-purpose 16-bit timers. Suitable for basic signal output, though care must be taken to ensure the 16-bit limit (\(65,535\)) isn’t exceeded when selecting prescaler values.Best Choice & Easy GPIO Pin MappingTIM2 Channel 1 mapping to PA0 (Pin A0 on the NUCLEO-L432KC board) is the best choice.Why: TIM2 is a 32-bit timer, giving you maximum resolution for calculating duty cycles and frequencies.Pin Connection: PA0 supports AF1 (Alternate Function 1) for TIM2_CH1. It breaks out cleanly on the outer headers of the Nucleo-32 board.Relevant FormulasOutput Frequency (\(f_{\text{PWM}}\))\[f_{\text{PWM}} = \frac{f_{\text{TIMCLK}}}{(\text{PSC} + 1) \times (\text{ARR} + 1)}\]\(f_{\text{TIMCLK}}\): Timer input clock frequency (e.g., \(80\text{ MHz}\) standard system clock).\(\text{PSC}\): Prescaler register value (\(0\text{ to }65,535\)).\(\text{ARR}\): Auto-Reload Register value (defines the period).Duty Cycle (%)\[\text{Duty Cycle} = \frac{\text{CCR1}}{\text{ARR} + 1} \times 100\%\]\(\text{CCR1}\): Capture/Compare Register value (controls when the pin toggles).Calculation Example (\(f_{\text{TIMCLK}} = 80\text{ MHz}\))To maintain a high counter resolution, pick a fixed Prescaler (\(\text{PSC}\)) value:Set \(\text{PSC} = 79\) (Divides the clock down to \(1\text{ MHz}\) or \(1\mu\text{s}\) per tick).For \(1000\text{ Hz}\) Output:\[\text{ARR} = \frac{80,000,000}{(79 + 1) \times 1000} - 1 = \frac{1,000,000}{1000} - 1 = 999\]For \(220\text{ Hz}\) Output:\[\text{ARR} = \frac{1,000,000}{220} - 1 \approx 4544\]Required Registers & Configuration StepsTo output a hardware PWM signal on pin PA0 without CPU overhead, configure the following registers:Clock Enable RegistersRCC-&gt;AHB2ENR: Set bit GPIOAEN to enable the GPIOA clock.RCC-&gt;APB1ENR1: Set bit TIM2EN to enable the TIM2 peripheral clock.GPIO Pin Configuration (GPIOA)GPIOA-&gt;MODER: Set MODER0 to 10 (Alternate Function Mode).GPIOA-&gt;AFR[0]: Set AFSEL0 to 0001 (AF1) to select TIM2_CH1.Timer Configuration (TIM2)TIM2-&gt;PSC: Set to 79 (1 MHz counter clock).TIM2-&gt;ARR: Set to desired frequency target (e.g., 999 for 1 kHz, 4544 for 220 Hz).TIM2-&gt;CCR1: Set to half of ARR for a 50% square wave (e.g., 500 for 1 kHz).TIM2-&gt;CCMR1:Set OC1M bits to 0110 (PWM Mode 1: Active while CNT &lt; CCR1).Set OC1PE bit to 1 (Enable Preload register).TIM2-&gt;CCER: Set CC1E bit to 1 to enable output on Channel 1.TIM2-&gt;CR1: Set CEN bit to 1 to enable the counter.:::For clarity, the prompt was as follows:What timers should I use on the STM32L432KC to generate frequencies ranging from 220Hz to 1kHz? What’s the best choice of timer if I want to easily connect it to a GPIO pin? What formulae are relevant, and what registers need to be set to configure them properly?I decided to prompt Gemini via the Harvey Mudd license (as to not train the AI) and I was presented with the “Initial Prompt” block seen above. Notably, it is comprehensive and covers a lot about timer selection and some of the information I cam across reading the manual. The AI does conclude that TIM2 is ideal for practically the same reason I described about it being 32-bit. However, it instead opts for pin A0 because it supports Alternate Function 1. I instead went the route of using GPIO B and just declaring it as a general purpose output but I can see how PA0 also makes sense and maybe even be more effective since it’s not paired with an LED and AF1 seems to be well equipped for this purpose. It also uses the same logic to enable the clock registers but has to use GPIOA-&gt;MODER and declare that as Alternate Function mode. When giving it the files, it functioned more as a table of contents with a summary rather than pointing me to specific addresses and telling me what to do about those sections. I can see why this may be helpful though.
?@fig-aisynthesis shows a screenshot of the second prompting and it seems pretty thorough as an overview if someone wanted to figure out where to go.ResourcesThe source code for the project can be found in the associated Github repository.

Design and Testing Methodology
The overall logic was stored in four modules where the top module called on the counter, decoder, and scanner submodules. It also included the assign logic prescribing which digit to select (based on the count declared in the top module) and assigned LEDs to be the inverse of the column inputs in the scanner. On the topic of the scanner submodule, it contained a counter in it of itself (targetting that 2 Hz) and contained logic to scan a 4 x 4 keypad by setting a 4-bit row vector to exhibit HIGH across a bit, one at a time. As previously stated, the parameterized clock divider in the top module was 18 bits whereas here, it increased to 25 bits (lower frequency naturally).






