module scanner#(
parameter WIDTH = 25,
parameter MAX = ~25'b0)
(
input logic int_osc, nreset, en,
output logic [3:0] row
);
    logic [WIDTH-1:0] keypadCount;
    winker #(WIDTH, MAX) keypad(.int_osc (int_osc), .nreset (nreset), .en (en), .count (keypadCount));
    assign row = 1'b1 &lt;&lt; (keypadCount[WIDTH-1:WIDTH-2]); //For the sake of parameterization

endmodule



For further elaboration, the scanner module was paramterized for the purpose of decreasing the clock division so an internally generated clock can properly exhibit the expected waveforms.

Block Diagram
{#fig-block-diagram}
The block diagram in ?@fig-block-diagram demonstrates the overall architecture of the design. The top-level module lab2_ea includes three submodules: the winker/counter which is paramterized for displaying a range of frequencies to the top module or scanner (winker), the scanner which cycles between which bit of the [3:0] row output vector (fed into the keypad) is HIGH (scanner), and the 7-segment display logic module (decoder) Additionally, HS_OSC is also leveraged for an internal 48 MHz clock.

Schematic
{#fig-schematic}
?@fig-schematic shows the electical layout of the design. Because driving every segment at 10 mA is strenuous (especially if we consider a case where both digits display 8), 150 \(\Omega\) was the appropriate value per the equation where \(R_{seg} = (3.3V-2)/10mA\) making 150 our ideal choice. Additionally, the transistors and the resistor values associated with the keypad scanner were also paramount. First, to explain, the display was multiplexed by having 2 independent 2N3906 PNP transistors control a common anode of each digit respectively (specifically the Collector pin of the transistor). These transistors are configured such that they saturate when the FPGA pin (connected to Base) reads LOW. The Emitter is simply connected to +3.3V. Another thing to clarify is the NPN setup for each row of the keypad (4 row inputs). Following the E155 site instructions, the Emitter was grounded to the FPGA ground, the Base was connected to an FPGA pin (row outputs) via a resistor (\(R_{base}\)). The Collector was wired to the rows of the keypad as shown in ?@fig-schematic and the columns were wired both to the FPGA as column inputs and to +3.3V via a 1 k\(\Omega\) pullup resistor. The value of this resistor is somewhat arbitrary and should be a decent value to pull-up. However, it does inform the value of \(R_{base}\) as stated earlier. The equation is practically \(R_{base} = (3.3V-0.7V)\) where 0.7V is the voltage drop across the resistor divided by \(I_{base}\) which we expect to be \(I_{collector}/10\) or 0.33mA. This ultimately yields a value of 7.8 k\(\Omega\) though a 2.2 k\(\Omega\) serves our purpose of saturating just fine.Results and DiscussionThe overall design and construction was successful as confirmed via simulation (incoming) and through the physical circuitry. I was able to accomplish all tasks outlined in the beginning of this lab report and utilize some interesting code and pin assignment workarounds. Overall, results are as expected and even with a few roadblocks in testbench logic (once again, incoming) and pins being assigned or not reading values.

Conclusion
The design successfully programmed the 7-segment display and outputted the right LED logic. I learned a lot about testbenches, communication between modules, etc.
